      {
        "id": "p28-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "For how many values of $n$ from 1 to 100 inclusive is $\\frac{1}{n}$ a terminating decimal?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$15$ values.",
          "worked": "Since $\\frac{1}{n}$ is already irreducible, its decimal terminates exactly when $n=2^a5^b$, where $a,b$ are non-negative integers.\n\nFor $n\\le100$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}b=0:&\\quad a=0,1,\\ldots,6 &&\\text{(7 values)}\\\\b=1:&\\quad a=0,1,\\ldots,4 &&\\text{(5 values)}\\\\b=2:&\\quad a=0,1,2 &&\\text{(3 values)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor $b\\ge3$, $5^b\\ge125>100$. Unique prime factorisation ensures that no values are counted twice. The total is $7+5+3=15$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q3b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "For how many values of $n$ from 1 to 1000 inclusive is $\\frac{1}{n}$ a terminating decimal?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$29$ values.",
          "worked": "The decimal terminates exactly when $n=2^a5^b$, where $a,b$ are non-negative integers.\n\nFor $n\\le1000$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}b=0:&\\quad a=0,1,\\ldots,9 &&\\text{(10 values)}\\\\b=1:&\\quad a=0,1,\\ldots,7 &&\\text{(8 values)}\\\\b=2:&\\quad a=0,1,\\ldots,5 &&\\text{(6 values)}\\\\b=3:&\\quad a=0,1,2,3 &&\\text{(4 values)}\\\\b=4:&\\quad a=0 &&\\text{(1 value)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor $b\\ge5$, $5^b\\ge3125>1000$. Unique prime factorisation ensures that no values are counted twice. The total is $10+8+6+4+1=29$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
